WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.550
Your first cream, your batch formula, and a facial oil.

2
00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:07.360
This is the video for module two of Natural Skincare.

3
00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:15.220
First you will make an oil-in-water cream. The method is the same for 100 g or 5 kg.

4
00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:21.720
Then you will write a formula in percentages and convert it to grams for any batch size.

5
00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:26.470
And you will blend a facial oil that resists going rancid.

6
00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:34.050
Weigh the water phase and oil phase into two jugs. Write down the weight of the water jug with its water.

7
00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:45.030
Heat both jugs in a water bath. The water phase goes to about 75 degrees, and the oil phase to the same, until the wax is fully melted.

8
00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:55.880
Hold the water at that temperature for 20 minutes. Then dry the jug, weigh it again, and add back hot distilled water to replace what was lost as steam.

9
00:00:57.130 --> 00:01:09.380
With both at a similar temperature, pour the oil into the water. Keep the blender head fully under the surface, and blend about a minute. Then rest about ten minutes, and blend again as it thickens.

10
00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:22.170
When it cools to about 45 degrees, add the preservative and mix very well. Fill sanitised containers and leave the lids slightly open until it reaches room temperature.

11
00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:28.470
Judge the texture after 24 hours, because creams keep thickening as they cool.

12
00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:38.900
For example, Chioma in Owerri makes a 200 g practice cream. Her water jug with the water phase weighs 412.40 g.

13
00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:46.590
At 75 degrees she starts a 20-minute timer, and keeps it between 73 and 77.

14
00:01:47.840 --> 00:02:00.510
Afterwards the jug weighs 405.10 g, so she adds 7.30 g of hot distilled water. Without that, her cream would be about 7 g short of water.

15
00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:13.540
After several blend-cool-blend rounds, at 44 degrees she adds 1.00 g of preservative. Her cream is smooth and white, and she uses it herself only.

16
00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:26.350
Write recipes in percentages, so any batch size comes out the same. For example, Rukayat in Ilorin wants five 50 g jars. That is a 250 g batch.

17
00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:39.740
Her learning formula has 70.5 percent distilled water. 250 times 70.5 divided by 100 is 176.25 g.

18
00:02:40.990 --> 00:02:45.360
Emulsifying wax at 6 percent is 15.00 g.

19
00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:58.600
The preservative at 0.5 percent is 1.25 g. That is why you need a 0.01 g scale. Always weigh, never measure by spoon or cup.

20
00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:09.170
Every line adds up to 250.00 g. Some cream always stays on the jug, so add a 5 to 10 percent allowance.

21
00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:17.460
Too much preservative can irritate skin, and too little leaves it under-protected. Stay inside the published range.

22
00:03:18.710 --> 00:03:26.970
A facial oil has no water, so no preservative. A water-based serum, with aloe or a hydrosol, needs one like any cream.

23
00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:36.900
The risk for oils is rancidity: a sharp smell like crayon or paint. Build most of the blend on a stable, light base such as jojoba.

24
00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:56.690
For example, Efe in Benin City uses jojoba 50 percent, baobab 30 percent, rosehip 19.5 percent, and vitamin E 0.5 percent. Oils weigh roughly 0.9 g per ml, so a 30 ml bottle needs 27 g.

25
00:03:57.940 --> 00:04:01.800
Vitamin E slows rancidity, but it is not a preservative.

26
00:04:03.050 --> 00:04:15.760
Fill amber or cobalt blue glass close to the top, and store it away from sunlight. Leave essential oils out of your first face oil, because some, like bergamot, can make skin react to sunlight.

27
00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:27.910
Jugs at 75 degrees are hot enough to burn. Lift them with oven gloves, pour slowly onto a stable surface, and keep the blender cable away from the water bath.

28
00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:36.520
No water does not mean no hygiene. Sanitise with 70 percent alcohol, and let everything dry completely.

29
00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:44.470
And do not give or sell any cream until you have completed the stability and preservative checks in Module 3.

30
00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:55.790
To remember. Heat to about 75 degrees and hold the water 20 minutes. Top up lost water by weight. Add the preservative at about 45.

31
00:04:56.140 --> 00:05:04.910
Grams equal batch size times percent divided by 100. And for oils, use a stable base, vitamin E and dark glass.

32
00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:14.760
Now make a 100 g practice cream with a timeline sheet, build your master formula sheet, and blend and record your first facial oil.

33
00:05:15.110 --> 00:05:17.050
Continue with the lessons below.
